      "id": 1,
      "text": "The name 'Ahwahnee' comes from a Miwok word meaning 'grassy valley'.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ahwahnee%2C_California",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Ahwahnee grew in the 1890s as a stagecoach stop on the Raymond–Wawona road.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ahwahnee%2C_California",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1899, Washburn and William M. built the two‑story Ahwahnee Tavern for travelers heading to Yosemite.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ahwahnee%2C_California",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "From 1919 to 1969, Ahwahnee hosted the Tri‑County Tuberculosis Sanatorium, a regional treatment center.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ahwahnee%2C_California",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Today, Ahwahnee Hills Regional Park occupies the former sanatorium grounds.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
